      <h1>Withdrawn: “Reading logs like an attacker”</h1>
      <div class="blog-post-meta">
        <span class="blog-post-date">Withdrawn August 29, 2026</span>
        <span class="blog-post-tag">correction</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="blog-article-lede">The original article has been removed because it turned synthetic log clues into confident stories about attacker identity and intent.</p>
    <div class="blog-body">
      <h2>What was wrong</h2>
      <p>Status codes, response sizes, timing, paths, and user-agent strings are evidence points. On their own, they do not prove that a resource exists, identify a person or tool, establish password reuse, reveal lockout knowledge, or demonstrate successful command execution. The article repeatedly crossed that line.</p>
      <p>Its documentation-range addresses and constructed events were useful as illustrations, but the prose treated them like definitive incident findings. It also made first-person incident-response claims that I cannot substantiate with publishable case material.</p>
      <h2>What would be required to republish it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Clearly labeled synthetic examples and competing explanations for each signal.</li>
        <li>A documented evidence ladder from observation to hypothesis to validated finding.</li>
        <li>Correlation with application, identity, endpoint, network, and change records.</li>
        <li>No personal incident claim without an attributable, appropriately sanitized artifact.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Log review is valuable precisely because it preserves uncertainty until multiple sources support a conclusion. The original post did not do that consistently.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</div>

